* **(B) Observe which types of flowers the birds with longer beaks prefer to visit.** While this might provide some insight, it doesn't directly test if longer beaks *lead* to better access to food within those flowers. Other factors could be at play.
* **(D) Compare the number of offspring produced by birds with long beaks versus birds with short beaks.** This looks at reproductive success, which could be influenced by many factors beyond beak length (e.g., mate selection, territory).

Option (C) is the most controlled experiment, allowing the scientist to isolate the effect of beak length on feeding success. 






